From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: allocate capture from a contiguous pool

DROPPED FROM THE SERIES 2026-09-01, OUT OF SERIES, DO NOT REAPPLY BY DEFAULT.

The premise below -- that DECD must fetch physical addresses -- was only true
because master 2 was left bypassing. Patches 0075 + 0076 flip master 2 to IOMMU
translation at the first frame, and a physically fragmented Cedrus CAPTURE
buffer then displays correctly: 300 moving frames at 27.12 fps from a buffer
with 56 physical breaks and a 32 KiB longest run, zero faults.

So this costs a permanent 64 MiB DRAM reservation to solve a problem that no
longer exists. It remains hardware-proven on a bypass kernel and is kept only as
a fallback control for that configuration; on a 0075+0076 kernel it is dead
weight.

One measurement trap it leaves behind: with this pool in place the `PHYS` line
printed by decd-play reports `contiguous=yes breaks=0` from *zero* resolved
pages (`absent=338`), because reserved no-map memory has no struct page for
pagemap to report. Contiguity numbers from a 0074 build prove nothing. See
docs/reference/iommu-runtime-flip-ordering-2026-09-01.md.

DECD has no scatter-gather and, in the adopted-display configuration, fetches
physical addresses without IOMMU translation.  Cedrus CAPTURE buffers exported
from its usual IOMMU-backed allocation can have scattered physical backing even
though they are contiguous in the VE's IOVA space.  Passing one of those buffers
to DECD makes it read beyond the first physical run.

Give the H713 VE a dedicated 64 MiB reusable CMA pool and force physical
contiguity for Cedrus CAPTURE allocations.  Keep OUTPUT allocations unchanged:
only decoded surfaces are exported to the physical-address-only consumer, and
forcing compressed input buffers contiguous would waste the finite pool.

The pool alone is insufficient while the VE is behind an IOMMU, because the DMA
allocator may otherwise satisfy a contiguous IOVA with scattered pages.
DMA_ATTR_FORCE_CONTIGUOUS makes the allocation come from one physical run.  The
DECD import guard added separately remains the runtime acceptance check that the
export maps as one complete DMA segment.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
@@ -65,6 +65,13 @@
 			no-map;
 		};
 
+		cedrus_cma: video-codec-pool {
+			compatible = "shared-dma-pool";
+			reusable;
+			size = <0x04000000>;
+			alignment = <0x00200000>;
+		};
+
 		/*
 		 * MIPS co-processor firmware and working memory
 		 * Layout from factory Android analysis:
@@ -1286,5 +1293,6 @@
 			 */
 			iommus = <&iommu 0 1>, <&iommu 1 1>;
+			memory-region = <&cedrus_cma>;
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
  * Marek Szyprowski, <m.szyprowski@samsung.com>
  */
 
+#include <linux/dma-mapping.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 
 #include <media/videobuf2-dma-contig.h>
@@ -720,6 +721,7 @@ int cedrus_queue_init(void *priv, struct vb2_queue *src_vq,
 	dst_vq->buf_struct_size = sizeof(struct cedrus_buffer);
 	dst_vq->ops = &cedrus_qops;
 	dst_vq->mem_ops = &vb2_dma_contig_memops;
+	dst_vq->dma_attrs = DMA_ATTR_FORCE_CONTIGUOUS;
 	dst_vq->timestamp_flags = V4L2_BUF_FLAG_TIMESTAMP_COPY;
 	dst_vq->lock = &ctx->dev->dev_mutex;
 	dst_vq->dev = ctx->dev->dev;
